{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And My name HaShem I did not make known to them.</b> Rashi's comment: 'It does not say לא הודעתי (I did not inform them)' etc., up to the end — after it was not said, 'and My name HaShem they did not ask of Me.' And if you ask: did He not inform them that such is His name? etc. — that is, and if you ask, the midrash can still be settled. And the latter statement also refers to what Rashi explained: 'And when Abraham sought a burial place' etc., up to 'and they did not question My ways.' And likewise the interpretation: and if you ask, when I spoke with them to give them the land, I did not make known to them My unique name, which implies He is faithful in His promises, and I did not converse with them in it the way I did with you; even so they did not question Me, but believed Me in mere speech. And now you will interpret לא נודעתי as לא הודעתי (I did not make known). The answer to this: behold, at the beginning, when He appeared to Abraham, He said, אני ה׳ (I am HaShem), and since He informed them His unique name and spoke with them in it, what reason did they have to question Him, and what reason would the Holy One, blessed be He, have to praise them? Another interpretation: and if you ask, He did not inform them that such is His name — that is, even though it was not said, 'and My name HaShem they did not ask of Me,' the derashah can be settled. And this is what it says: many times I appeared to them as El Shaddai, and I did not make known to them My unique name, and even so they did not ask Me, 'What is Your name?' — for since I had not made known My name to them, they should have asked, 'What is Your name?' and even so they did not ask it. And you said, 'What is His name? What shall I say to them?' The answer to this: behold, at the beginning, when He appeared to Abraham, it was said, אני ה׳ etc., and since He informed them that such is His name, what reason did they have to ask what His name was, for it is not a person's way to ask about what he already knows, and what reason would the Holy One, blessed be He, have to praise them? And further, Rashi commented: how is the sequence connected in these matters etc. — that is, for the first interpretation it works fine: because I established and upheld a covenant to give their seed the land, therefore I too have heard etc.; but for the latter interpretation, what is the relevance of וגם אני שמעתי to the covenant I established? Was it because the patriarchs did not hold it during their lifetimes that He needed to hear the groan of the children of Israel? Therefore I say the verse can be settled etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "This is a dense, multi-layered comment compiling multiple objections and answers related to Rashi's two interpretations. The repeated וא״ת, ע״א, and הכי קאמר markers are preserved. The phrase לוגם appears to be a corruption of לבריתי; translated hyper-literally as 'to the covenant' based on context. The abbreviation וכו׳ is rendered 'etc.' throughout."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:56:42.530094992Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}